From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0242] fix(iokit): test the restart bit as a bit, not the whole bool

0241 put a third bit (kLoopThreadNamed) in the byte loopRestart occupies, on
the grounds that every access to that byte already went through the byte-wide
ISSETP/SETP/CLRP helpers. One access did not. runEventSources() tested its own
state as

    } else if (fFlags & kLoopRestart) {

and fFlags is the bool loopRestart itself, so `x & 1` is `x != 0` as far as the
C++ type system goes and the compiler is entitled to drop the mask. It did: in
the 0135 object the restart test, at runEventSources()+0x1a8, is

    +0x1a8:  ldrb    w8, [x19, #0x39]    ; loopRestart
    +0x1ac:  tbnz    w8, #0x1, <abort>   ; ISSETP(&fFlags, kLoopTerminate)
    +0x1b0:  orr     w28, w28, w22       ; the source's answer joins `more`
    +0x1b4:  cbnz    w8, <scan start>    ; fFlags & kLoopRestart, folded

so the scan restarts whenever that byte is non-zero, not when bit 0 is set.
With 0100 in it, every pass set `more = true` and broke out of the event-source
chain, `do { ... } while (more)` ran again immediately, and the loop never
completed a pass or went back to sleep.

The fold is not new to 0135: the 0133 kernel carries the same four instructions
at the same address, and it booted, because that byte only ever held the two
state bits and bit 1 is tested first. The third bit is what turns an unnoticed
fold into a hang - which is why the spelling, not the feature, is the bug.

On the device that is the 0135 hang: the console stopped on
"SWEET IOWORKLOOP: thread 0xfffffe2000fb4430 running loop 0xfffffe24cd5ec900"
with the touch registration rows above it and nothing after, which is the line
threadMain() prints before handing the loop to that first scan; the UFS ring was
byte-identical to the previous boot's, so the machine had not survived the ten
seconds to its first flush. The loop it stopped on is the one the boot thread
was waiting for, and it was spinning instead.

Bisected on the device with two binary patches of the same CI kernel, changing
only the four instructions in front of the print: naming the thread on every
wakeup while also setting the bit hung the boot; naming it on every wakeup and
writing no bit at all reached the desktop. 0133 (no third bit, no such test)
booted; 0135 hung twice. The kernel objects of the two builds differ in exactly
two archive members, IOWorkLoop.cpo and __.SYMDEF, and the only source
difference is this feature.

The fix is the one-line spelling the two neighbours already use: ISSETP takes
the address as unsigned char *, so the compiler cannot fold the mask away and
emits tbnz w8, #0x0 - it also merges with the terminate test into a single
load. The comment above the enum now carries the rule, because it is not
obvious from `bool`: any bit beyond the two state bits makes every `fFlags &`
in this file load-bearing, so readers must use the helpers.

tools/tests/test-mh2lm-workloop-log.py grows two guards that would have caught
this before the image was flashed: the shipped text may name fFlags only inside
ISSETP/SETP/CLRP (the 0135 line is rejected with the offending source line),
and both spellings of the restart test are compiled for arm64 and disassembled,
requiring a bit-0 branch for the shipped form and a non-zero branch for the
folded one, so the trap is documented by a failure that names it.

diff --git a/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/IOWorkLoop.cpp b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/IOWorkLoop.cpp
index d160498..b351f44 100644
--- a/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/IOWorkLoop.cpp
+++ b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/IOWorkLoop.cpp
@@ -68,7 +68,18 @@ OSMetaClassDefineReservedUnused(IOWorkLoop, 7);
  * as the two state bits (loopRestart is a bool and ISSETP/SETP/CLRP already
  * test it as a byte), it is set on the loop's own object so twelve loops each
  * print once instead of sharing one remembered thread, and it is cleared when
- * the loop thread exits so a loop that gains a new owner announces it again. */
+ * the loop thread exits so a loop that gains a new owner announces it again.
+ *
+ * Because a third bit rides this byte, every reader must go through ISSETP.
+ * Spelled `fFlags & kLoopRestart` the compiler knows the object is a bool, so
+ * `x & 1` is `x != 0` and it emits cbnz: any other bit in the byte then reads
+ * as "restart requested", the event-source scan never completes a pass, and
+ * whichever thread waits on that loop never runs again.  The 0135 image did
+ * exactly that and stopped at its first loop with half a page of console and a
+ * ring that had not reached its first flush; runEventSources() now tests the
+ * restart bit through ISSETP like kLoopTerminate next to it (one load for
+ * both), and tools/tests/test-mh2lm-workloop-log.py compiles both spellings to
+ * keep the trap out. */
 enum IOWorkLoopState {
 	kLoopRestart = 0x1,
 	kLoopTerminate = 0x2,
@@ -427,7 +438,7 @@ IOWorkLoop::runEventSources()
 
 			if (ISSETP(&fFlags, kLoopTerminate)) {
 				goto abort;
-			} else if (fFlags & kLoopRestart) {
+			} else if (ISSETP(&fFlags, kLoopRestart)) {
 				more = true;
 				break;
 			}
